{{NZelectorateShortDesc|current=yes|type=general|region=Auckland}}
'''Epsom''' is a [[New Zealand electorates|New Zealand electorate]] in [[Auckland]], returning one Member of Parliament to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. Since the [[2014 New Zealand general election|2014 general election]], Epsom has been represented by [[David Seymour]], the leader of the [[ACT New Zealand|ACT Party]].

Epsom has been an important electorate in New Zealand politics as, since 2005, it has allowed the ACT Party to gain seats in parliament without meeting the five percent party vote [[Electoral threshold|threshold]] as the party leaders David Seymour, [[John Banks (New Zealand politician)|John Banks]] and [[Rodney Hide]] have each won the electorate.

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Epsom.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
Epsom is based around central and eastern [[Auckland isthmus]]. It contains the suburbs of [[Parnell, New Zealand|Parnell]], [[Remuera]], [[Mount Eden]], [[Newmarket, New Zealand|Newmarket]], half of [[Greenlane]] and the eponymous suburb of [[Epsom, New Zealand|Epsom]]. Under boundary changes resulting from the 2006 census, Epsom was enlarged to include the central city suburb of [[Grafton, New Zealand|Grafton]], but most of the area was lost again following the 2013 census. In the 2025 boundary review, the electorate would gain [[Grafton, New Zealand|Grafton]], [[Newton, New Zealand|Newton]] and [[Eden Terrace]] from [[Auckland Central (New Zealand electorate)|Auckland Central]] and lose [[Balmoral, New Zealand|Balmoral]] to [[Mount Albert (New Zealand electorate)|Mount Albert]], though Epsom would retain part of Balmoral after public consultation.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Boundary review objections released |url=https://elections.nz/media-and-news/2025/boundary-review-objections-released/ |access-date=16 September 2025 |website=Elections |language=en-NZ}}</ref><ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=16 September 2025 |page=19 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref> It is New Zealand's smallest electorate, covering just {{convert|20|km2|sqmi|0|abbr=on}}.<ref name="parllib" />

Epsom was created ahead of the first [[Mixed-member proportional representation|Mixed Member Proportional]] (MMP) election in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]], carved out of the [[Remuera (New Zealand electorate)|Remuera]] and [[Eden (New Zealand electorate)|Eden]] seats. Remuera was a safe seat for the National Party, having never elected a member of parliament from the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]], while Eden was a [[Bellwether (politics)|bellwether]] seat, changing hands with the change of government. Both of these seats were held by National MPs – [[Christine Fletcher]] in Eden and [[Doug Graham (New Zealand politician)|Doug (later Sir Douglas) Graham]] in Remuera.

The electorate's population is predominantly [[European New Zealanders]] with a significant [[Asian New Zealanders|Asian]] population. The electorate has the highest proportion of people earning over $150,000 a year in all New Zealand electorates, per the 2018 census.<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/epsom-electorate-profile/ |title=Epsom – Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 16 September 2025}}</ref>

==History==
The Epsom seat was first contested in New Zealand's first MMP election in [[1996 New Zealand general election|1996]]. The National party candidate was [[Christine Fletcher]]; she came out of the election with the nation's biggest personal majority: a 19,000 vote margin over the second placed [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] candidate, [[Helen Duncan (politician)|Helen Duncan]].

With Fletcher standing down at the [[1999 New Zealand general election|1999]] election to focus on her role as the newly elected [[Mayor of Auckland]], the electorate battle was a contest between new National candidate [[Richard Worth]] and ACT List MP [[Rodney Hide]]. Worth won the seat by approximately 1,900 votes. In [[2002 New Zealand general election|2002]], he easily retained Epsom, with other parties contesting only the party vote.

The [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005]] race for Epsom was won by Rodney Hide<ref name="Hon Rodney Hide">{{cite web|title=Hon Rodney Hide |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/7/6/1/49MP11981-Hide-Rodney.htm |publisher=New Zealand Parliament|access-date=26 November 2011}}</ref> after a tough contest for the personal vote. As the leader of ACT, Hide was determined to contest Epsom in order to guarantee his party's representation in the next parliament, should ACT not break the five percent [[Election threshold|threshold]] – under New Zealand electoral law, a party can gain representation by either getting five percent of the vote or by winning one or more electoral seats.

As it became more likely ACT would not break five percent, the campaign in Epsom became more intense, with Hide lobbying voters to vote [[Tactical voting|strategically]] to keep ACT in Parliament, a message that ultimately prevailed, with National MP Richard Worth, defeated by 3,102 votes on election night and returned to Parliament via the National Party list. Hide's win in Epsom also allowed ACT member [[Heather Roy]] to enter parliament.

Hide increased his majority in 2008, and winning Epsom allowed four other ACT MPs to enter parliament. But Hide stepped down as ACT leader in April 2011 after succumbing to a leadership challenge from Don Brash.<ref name="3news">{{cite web|url=http://www.3news.co.nz/Rodney-Hide-resigns-makes-way-for-Brash/tabid/419/articleID/208855/Default.aspx|title=Rodney Hide resigns, makes way for Brash|work=[[3 News]]|date=27 April 2011|first=Dan|last=Satherley|access-date=26 November 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121001221323/http://www.3news.co.nz/Rodney-Hide-resigns-makes-way-for-Brash/tabid/419/articleID/208855/Default.aspx|archive-date=1 October 2012|url-status=dead}}</ref> The ACT party selected former Auckland Mayor [[John Banks (New Zealand politician)|John Banks]] as their candidate for the [[2011 New Zealand general election|2011 election]], who won the contest.<ref name="2011 Epsom">{{cite web |title=Official Count Results – Epsom |url= http://www.electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2011/electorate-12.html |publisher=New Zealand Electoral Commission|access-date=15 December 2011}}</ref>

In 2013, John Banks announced that he would leave Parliament at the 2014 election, and so would not contest the Epsom electorate. After being found guilty at trial for electoral fraud, he announced his resignation effective 13 June 2014, leaving the Epsom seat vacant.<ref name="Banks resignation">{{Cite news|title=John Banks to resign from Parliament | url=http://www.nzherald.co.nz/politics/news/article.cfm?c_id=280&objectid=11270036 |work=The New Zealand Herald | author=Audrey Young |date=8 June 2014 |access-date=10 June 2014}}</ref> Due to the proximity of the [[2014 New Zealand general election|next general election]], Parliament voted by [[supermajority]] to avoid a by-election.<ref name="Byelection">{{cite web |title=Parliament avoids calling by-election |date= 18 June 2014 |url= http://www.radionz.co.nz/news/political/247573/parliament-avoids-calling-by-election |publisher=Radio New Zealand |access-date=18 June 2014}}</ref>

